<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Júcar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Xúquer, Sucro</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Júcar (Xúquer in Valencian) is a river of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, about 500 kilometres long, rising in the Montes Universales and flowing through the province of Cuenca and across the Valencian plain to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> at Cullera. Known in antiquity as the Sucro, it marked the southern limit of the Edetani. Its waters irrigate the orange groves and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> fields of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jucar.jpg" alt="Júcar" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 19Tarrestnom65 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cubas._Paisaje._Wiki_takes_La_Manchuela_15.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
